.TH CB_NEW_KV 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
cb_new_kv \- Create a new key-value pair in a buffer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/cb_internal.h>
.IB #include <lib/byte.h>

.IB size_t
.IB cb_new_kv (
.IB const char* key,
.IB size_t keylen,
.IB const void* value,
.IB size_t len,
.IB void* out
.IB );

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcb_new_kv\fR function creates a new key-value pair in a buffer. The key and value are copied into the buffer, and the function returns the total length of the key-value pair, including the null terminator for the key.

The function takes five arguments:
.TP
.IR key (const char*)
A pointer to the buffer containing the key data.
.TP
.IR keylen (size_t)
The length of the key.
.TP
.IR value (const void*)
A pointer to the buffer containing the value data.
.TP
.IR len (size_t)
The length of the value.
.TP
.IR out (void*)
A pointer to the buffer where the key-value pair will be stored.

The function returns the total length of the key-value pair, including the null terminator for the key.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcb_new_kv\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/cb_internal.h>
#include <lib/byte.h>

int main() {
    char buffer[100];
    const char* key = "example_key";
    const char* value = "example_value";
    size_t keylen = 13;
    size_t vallen = 15;

    size_t length = cb_new_kv(key, keylen, value, vallen, buffer);

    printf("Buffer: %s\n", buffer);
    printf("Length: %zu\n", length);

    return 0;
}
.nef